1
00:00:00,070 --> 00:00:03,520
If you're a complete beginner who wants to learn trading, or

2
00:00:03,570 --> 00:00:06,790
you've already been trading for two or three years, I can

3
00:00:06,840 --> 00:00:09,900
guarantee that by the end of this video, if you watch

4
00:00:09,950 --> 00:00:13,600
it all the way through, you'll have a much clearer understanding

5
00:00:13,650 --> 00:00:18,290
of how to mark trading zones and identify the right levels.

6
00:00:18,340 --> 00:00:21,470
I'm going to explain everything, from the basics to

7
00:00:21,520 --> 00:00:25,220
more advanced concepts, so you can understand zone marking

8
00:00:25,270 --> 00:00:28,460
even if you're trading on the one-minute timeframe.

9
00:00:28,510 --> 00:00:32,250
If you already have one or two years of experience, my

10
00:00:32,300 --> 00:00:36,540
suggestion is to watch the whole video from start to finish.

11
00:00:36,590 --> 00:00:39,620
I believe this will be a useful revision for you.

12
00:00:39,670 --> 00:00:40,980
Don't skip ahead.

13
00:00:41,030 --> 00:00:42,960
You might learn something new.

14
00:00:43,010 --> 00:00:45,940
Let's talk about marking zones.

15
00:00:45,990 --> 00:00:47,860
A lot of people have asked me, can

16
00:00:47,910 --> 00:00:49,830
you make a video on marking zones?”

17
00:00:49,880 --> 00:00:51,930
How do we mark zones?

18
00:00:51,980 --> 00:00:54,810
How do we identify levels where a move has

19
00:00:54,860 --> 00:00:58,360
started, or where the next move could start?

20
00:00:58,410 --> 00:01:04,560
First, if you're a complete beginner, let's cover basic support and resistance.

21
00:01:04,610 --> 00:01:06,670
What is resistance?

22
00:01:06,720 --> 00:01:09,880
Resistance is a price level where the market repeatedly

23
00:01:09,930 --> 00:01:14,040
moves up, meets selling pressure, and then falls.

24
00:01:14,090 --> 00:01:16,950
It's usually easy to spot on a chart.

25
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:18,600
Look at this example.

26
00:01:18,650 --> 00:01:21,610
Price reaches this level once, twice, and

27
00:01:21,660 --> 00:01:24,840
three times, and falls each time.

28
00:01:24,890 --> 00:01:26,730
That's a resistance level.

29
00:01:26,780 --> 00:01:30,920
Whenever price reaches that area, it has been rejected.

30
00:01:30,970 --> 00:01:33,220
Support is the opposite.

31
00:01:33,270 --> 00:01:38,090
It's an area where price repeatedly comes down and then moves upward.

32
00:01:38,140 --> 00:01:42,130
Buyers are stepping in and trying to push price higher.

33
00:01:42,180 --> 00:01:46,380
That's the basic concept of support and resistance.

34
00:01:46,430 --> 00:01:49,560
So how do we spot these levels?

35
00:01:49,610 --> 00:01:52,210
We look for a price area that has been respected

36
00:01:52,260 --> 00:01:56,700
more than once, where price has reacted and moved away.

37
00:01:56,750 --> 00:02:00,260
If it has held two or three times, traders may watch

38
00:02:00,310 --> 00:02:04,290
it for another reaction on the third or fourth test.

39
00:02:04,340 --> 00:02:08,710
But how do we improve the probability of choosing a useful level?

40
00:02:08,760 --> 00:02:11,690
That's what we're going to explore in detail.

41
00:02:11,740 --> 00:02:14,410
Suppose I mark a resistance level because price

42
00:02:14,460 --> 00:02:17,200
has reached it three times and fallen.

43
00:02:17,250 --> 00:02:21,620
What guarantees that price will fall again on the fourth test?

44
00:02:21,670 --> 00:02:22,760
Nothing.

45
00:02:22,810 --> 00:02:26,490
Price could break above the resistance and keep moving higher.

46
00:02:26,540 --> 00:02:29,600
That's exactly what we need to understand.

47
00:02:29,650 --> 00:02:32,790
Now, here's the second basic concept.

48
00:02:32,840 --> 00:02:37,450
When price breaks resistance, that former resistance can become support.

49
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:43,450
And when price breaks support, that former support can become resistance.

50
00:02:43,500 --> 00:02:48,200
Let's say price repeatedly holds a support level and moves upward.

51
00:02:48,250 --> 00:02:51,040
Eventually, that support breaks.

52
00:02:51,090 --> 00:02:52,830
If price comes back to the same

53
00:02:52,880 --> 00:02:56,640
level, it may start acting as resistance.

54
00:02:56,690 --> 00:02:57,680
Look at this example.

55
00:02:57,730 --> 00:03:01,210
What used to be resistance has become support.

56
00:03:01,260 --> 00:03:04,310
Price has returned to that area, and we can

57
00:03:04,360 --> 00:03:07,150
watch to see whether buyers defend it again.

58
00:03:07,200 --> 00:03:09,710
Now let's look at the opposite.

59
00:03:09,760 --> 00:03:14,810
This level acted as support twice, but price eventually broke below it.

60
00:03:14,860 --> 00:03:20,120
When price returned to the same area, it met resistance and fell.

61
00:03:20,170 --> 00:03:22,480
The former support had become resistance.

62
00:03:22,540 --> 00:03:26,440
These are two very basic concepts.

63
00:03:26,490 --> 00:03:31,260
If you want to master zone marking, you need to understand them.

64
00:03:31,310 --> 00:03:35,630
But why does a broken support sometimes turn into resistance?

65
00:03:35,680 --> 00:03:38,670
What's the logic behind it?

66
00:03:38,720 --> 00:03:41,070
Let's imagine I'm a buyer.

67
00:03:41,120 --> 00:03:45,980
I've bought around 4,400 before, and price has gone up each time.

68
00:03:46,030 --> 00:03:50,140
I bought once at 4,400 and made a profit.

69
00:03:50,190 --> 00:03:55,100
I bought a second time and made a profit again.

70
00:03:55,150 --> 00:03:58,930
On the third visit, I think, “This is support.

71
00:03:58,980 --> 00:04:00,570
It has worked before.

72
00:04:00,620 --> 00:04:02,530
I'll buy at 4,400 again.”

73
00:04:02,580 --> 00:04:05,660
But this time, price breaks lower, and

74
00:04:05,710 --> 00:04:08,930
my position goes into a loss.

75
00:04:08,980 --> 00:04:13,080
When price finally returns to 4,400, what might I do?

76
00:04:13,130 --> 00:04:16,810
I may think, “I was stuck in that losing buy.

77
00:04:16,860 --> 00:04:20,200
Now that price is back at my entry, I'm getting out.”

78
00:04:20,250 --> 00:04:26,300
That selling pressure can help turn the old support into resistance.

79
00:04:26,350 --> 00:04:28,250
It's a simple way to understand one of

80
00:04:28,300 --> 00:04:32,780
the psychological reasons behind the support-to-resistance flip.

81
00:04:32,830 --> 00:04:37,340
The reverse can happen when resistance becomes support.

82
00:04:37,390 --> 00:04:41,460
Imagine price has repeatedly fallen from 5,600.

83
00:04:41,510 --> 00:04:45,930
You've been buying around 5,300 and selling around 5,600.

84
00:04:45,980 --> 00:04:50,290
It worked several times.

85
00:04:50,340 --> 00:04:54,390
Then you sell again at 5,600, expecting another drop.

86
00:04:54,440 --> 00:04:59,270
But instead, price breaks above the resistance.

87
00:04:59,320 --> 00:05:03,100
When price returns to 5,600, your thinking may change.

88
00:05:03,150 --> 00:05:06,450
You might decide, “That resistance has broken.

89
00:05:06,500 --> 00:05:09,930
There may be a better chance of price rising from here.”

90
00:05:09,980 --> 00:05:14,530
So instead of selling, you look for a buy.

91
00:05:14,580 --> 00:05:17,670
The area you once treated as resistance is now

92
00:05:17,720 --> 00:05:20,990
an area where you might look for support.

93
00:05:21,040 --> 00:05:26,600
That's how trader behavior and market psychology can contribute to these reactions.

94
00:05:26,650 --> 00:05:31,900
Of course, there are more technical factors and more advanced mechanisms involved.

95
00:05:31,950 --> 00:05:34,440
But this is an easy way to understand why

96
00:05:34,490 --> 00:05:39,380
support can become resistance, and resistance can become support.

97
00:05:39,430 --> 00:05:40,910
I hope that's clear.

98
00:05:40,960 --> 00:05:43,370
This is the basic foundation.

99
00:05:43,420 --> 00:05:46,960
Now we're going to start marking zones more precisely.

100
00:05:47,010 --> 00:05:50,600
So far, we've established that support and resistance are

101
00:05:50,650 --> 00:05:54,570
areas where price has reacted two or three times.

102
00:05:54,620 --> 00:05:59,390
But that is where many traders stop, and that's a mistake.

103
00:05:59,440 --> 00:06:01,540
When you're marking a zone, I want you

104
00:06:01,590 --> 00:06:05,070
to keep two important questions in mind.

105
00:06:05,120 --> 00:06:09,100
First, did price move away from the zone with strength?

106
00:06:09,150 --> 00:06:12,630
Was there a strong reaction or a meaningful move?

107
00:06:12,680 --> 00:06:15,550
Second, when price moved away from the zone,

108
00:06:15,600 --> 00:06:19,150
did it create a break of structure?

109
00:06:19,200 --> 00:06:22,780
I'll explain both concepts with an example.

110
00:06:22,830 --> 00:06:24,360
Look at this chart.

111
00:06:24,410 --> 00:06:26,550
Here we have a potential support level.

112
00:06:26,600 --> 00:06:30,560
Price has touched the area twice and moved higher.

113
00:06:30,610 --> 00:06:33,100
Many beginners would say, “Two touches?

114
00:06:33,150 --> 00:06:34,580
That's support.

115
00:06:34,630 --> 00:06:35,530
Done.”

116
00:06:35,580 --> 00:06:37,760
But that isn't enough.

117
00:06:37,810 --> 00:06:42,670
Ask yourself: Did a strong bullish move begin there?

118
00:06:42,720 --> 00:06:45,600
Support and resistance help us identify areas

119
00:06:45,650 --> 00:06:49,070
where buying and selling pressure may exist.

120
00:06:49,120 --> 00:06:50,880
But it's not enough just to know

121
00:06:50,930 --> 00:06:53,360
that buyers or sellers are present.

122
00:06:53,410 --> 00:06:54,610
We want to know whether they are

123
00:06:54,660 --> 00:06:58,260
strong enough to move price significantly.

124
00:06:58,310 --> 00:07:02,880
If buyers are present but weak, the support can still break.

125
00:07:02,930 --> 00:07:06,840
If buying pressure is strong, price may move sharply higher.

126
00:07:06,880 --> 00:07:10,140
That strength is what interests us.

127
00:07:10,190 --> 00:07:15,560
In this example, price reacted from support with a strong upward move.

128
00:07:15,610 --> 00:07:17,870
That's our first positive sign.

129
00:07:17,920 --> 00:07:20,580
Now, what about a break of structure?

130
00:07:20,620 --> 00:07:23,850
Did price move above a previous significant

131
00:07:23,900 --> 00:07:26,630
high and create a fresh higher high?

132
00:07:26,680 --> 00:07:27,600
Yes.

133
00:07:27,650 --> 00:07:29,660
It continued the uptrend and reached a

134
00:07:29,710 --> 00:07:32,460
level it had not reached before.

135
00:07:32,510 --> 00:07:36,480
That's what I'm referring to here as a bullish break of structure.

136
00:07:36,540 --> 00:07:39,730
If price had bounced a little and then fallen

137
00:07:39,780 --> 00:07:43,430
straight back, we wouldn't have the same confirmation.

138
00:07:43,480 --> 00:07:48,090
But in this example, the move from support broke a previous high.

139
00:07:48,140 --> 00:07:50,300
So we have both things: a strong

140
00:07:50,350 --> 00:07:52,980
reaction and a break of structure.

141
00:07:53,040 --> 00:07:57,440
Now we wait for price to return to that support zone.

142
00:07:57,490 --> 00:08:00,970
Here comes price, back into the zone.

143
00:08:01,020 --> 00:08:03,110
Suppose it prints a green candle.

144
00:08:03,160 --> 00:08:07,510
That gives me an entry confirmation to consider a buy.

145
00:08:07,560 --> 00:08:09,790
I could place the stop loss below the support

146
00:08:09,840 --> 00:08:13,290
zone and set a target near the previous high.

147
00:08:13,340 --> 00:08:16,370
If price has already broken structure to the upside from

148
00:08:16,420 --> 00:08:20,410
this area, it may be capable of another strong move.

149
00:08:20,460 --> 00:08:23,290
That's the idea behind this setup.

150
00:08:23,340 --> 00:08:25,950
Let's say my target is 4,500.

151
00:08:26,000 --> 00:08:27,340
I enter the buy.

152
00:08:27,390 --> 00:08:31,940
Price rises, pulls back, taps the same area again,

153
00:08:31,990 --> 00:08:35,020
and then makes a much bigger move upward.

154
00:08:35,070 --> 00:08:40,620
That's how I use strength and structure to filter support zones.

155
00:08:40,670 --> 00:08:44,070
Of course, we're looking at a higher timeframe here.

156
00:08:44,120 --> 00:08:46,440
Later, we'll bring the same logic down to

157
00:08:46,490 --> 00:08:50,150
a smaller timeframe and go into more detail.

158
00:08:50,200 --> 00:08:52,400
That was our support example.

159
00:08:52,450 --> 00:08:55,420
Now let's look at resistance.

160
00:08:55,470 --> 00:08:56,940
Look at this chart.

161
00:08:56,990 --> 00:09:00,740
If you're a complete beginner, it might seem confusing.

162
00:09:00,790 --> 00:09:05,050
You can see several possible levels: a former resistance that turned

163
00:09:05,100 --> 00:09:10,430
into support, another reaction zone here, and another one over there.

164
00:09:10,480 --> 00:09:14,740
You might be thinking, “Which level am I supposed to mark?”

165
00:09:14,790 --> 00:09:16,440
Let me simplify it.

166
00:09:16,490 --> 00:09:18,570
Forget the noise for a moment and ask

167
00:09:18,620 --> 00:09:22,730
one question: Where did the big move start?

168
00:09:22,780 --> 00:09:25,840
Where did the strongest downward momentum begin?

169
00:09:25,880 --> 00:09:29,820
Take a moment and look at the chart.

170
00:09:29,870 --> 00:09:33,190
If you chose this particular level, you're correct.

171
00:09:33,240 --> 00:09:36,190
Price was moving downward, but the strongest

172
00:09:36,240 --> 00:09:39,140
bearish move began from this area.

173
00:09:39,190 --> 00:09:41,780
If I want to mark just one important resistance

174
00:09:41,830 --> 00:09:45,090
level, this is the one I would prioritize.

175
00:09:45,140 --> 00:09:47,520
Price has reacted here multiple times.

176
00:09:47,570 --> 00:09:50,810
A strong downward move started here.

177
00:09:50,860 --> 00:09:53,280
And that move also broke structure to the

178
00:09:53,330 --> 00:09:56,690
downside by taking out a previous significant low.

179
00:09:56,740 --> 00:09:59,800
That's the combination we're looking for.

180
00:09:59,850 --> 00:10:02,030
Now, someone might say, “Dr.

181
00:10:02,080 --> 00:10:04,910
Aaron, what about this other level?

182
00:10:04,960 --> 00:10:10,420
It was support, became resistance, and price moved down from it too.”

183
00:10:10,470 --> 00:10:11,690
That's a fair question.

184
00:10:11,740 --> 00:10:13,860
But look carefully.

185
00:10:13,910 --> 00:10:16,980
Price tried to react at that lower level, moved

186
00:10:17,030 --> 00:10:20,710
down, failed to continue, and came back up.

187
00:10:20,760 --> 00:10:24,080
It's now approaching the higher resistance area.

188
00:10:24,130 --> 00:10:28,360
Does that mean price absolutely cannot fall from the lower level?

189
00:10:28,400 --> 00:10:29,210
No.

190
00:10:29,260 --> 00:10:31,860
But the move toward that level was strong,

191
00:10:31,910 --> 00:10:34,260
and it didn't hold the way we wanted.

192
00:10:34,310 --> 00:10:36,120
I would give more weight to the resistance

193
00:10:36,160 --> 00:10:39,720
zone from which the major bearish move began.

194
00:10:39,770 --> 00:10:41,810
Watch how price develops.

195
00:10:41,860 --> 00:10:44,070
It breaks through the lower level.

196
00:10:44,120 --> 00:10:47,210
Now the next important level left on our chart is

197
00:10:47,260 --> 00:10:51,910
the main resistance zone where the stronger downward move began.

198
00:10:51,960 --> 00:10:56,040
Remember this: when you're confused by several zones, give

199
00:10:56,090 --> 00:10:59,320
priority to the one that produced a significant move.

200
00:10:59,380 --> 00:11:04,090
If an area didn't create much momentum, don't automatically treat it as

201
00:11:04,140 --> 00:11:08,600
the most important level unless current price action gives you a reason.

202
00:11:08,660 --> 00:11:14,330
Suppose price now reaches our marked resistance and prints a bearish candle.

203
00:11:14,380 --> 00:11:17,980
That could be an opportunity to consider a short trade.

204
00:11:18,030 --> 00:11:20,720
Let's place the stop loss above the candle

205
00:11:20,770 --> 00:11:24,050
or above the zone, depending on our plan.

206
00:11:24,100 --> 00:11:27,780
We could target a risk-to-reward ratio of one to two.

207
00:11:27,820 --> 00:11:31,560
Even a smaller move can offer an opportunity.

208
00:11:31,610 --> 00:11:33,840
On gold, for example, a move of

209
00:11:33,890 --> 00:11:36,740
around 50 dollars can be substantial.

210
00:11:36,790 --> 00:11:41,150
The key is identifying the zone and planning the trade.

211
00:11:41,200 --> 00:11:44,830
Now, let's follow the example on the chart.

212
00:11:44,880 --> 00:11:47,860
Price has moved upward and tapped our resistance.

213
00:11:47,900 --> 00:11:50,550
A red candle appears.

214
00:11:50,600 --> 00:11:54,250
Let's say I enter a short, place the stop loss above the

215
00:11:54,300 --> 00:11:58,820
area, and set the target near the round number of 4,000.

216
00:11:58,870 --> 00:12:05,220
In this illustration, the risk-to-reward ratio is roughly one to 3.9.

217
00:12:05,270 --> 00:12:06,800
Let's see what happens.

218
00:12:06,850 --> 00:12:09,080
Price moves down, gets close to the

219
00:12:09,130 --> 00:12:12,840
target, pulls up briefly, then falls again.

220
00:12:12,890 --> 00:12:14,150
And there we go.

221
00:12:14,200 --> 00:12:16,470
Our target is hit.

222
00:12:16,520 --> 00:12:21,810
We used a simple principle: identify the important zone, look for strength

223
00:12:21,860 --> 00:12:27,380
and structure, wait for price to return, and then evaluate a trade.

224
00:12:27,430 --> 00:12:30,780
You can also apply this idea to swing trading.

225
00:12:30,830 --> 00:12:35,310
If you're working, studying, or can't watch charts all day, you may

226
00:12:35,360 --> 00:12:39,780
prefer higher-timeframe setups that can last for two or three days.

227
00:12:39,830 --> 00:12:43,760
Mark the important zones, place alerts, and

228
00:12:43,810 --> 00:12:46,240
wait for price to reach those areas.

229
00:12:46,290 --> 00:12:50,240
Then assess your entry, stop loss, and target.

230
00:12:50,290 --> 00:12:53,140
We're using a one-hour chart in this example, and

231
00:12:53,190 --> 00:12:55,900
this kind of planning can also be useful for

232
00:12:55,950 --> 00:12:59,290
traders who cannot monitor every small movement.

233
00:12:59,340 --> 00:13:03,500
Now let's move on to more advanced examples: support

234
00:13:03,550 --> 00:13:08,330
turning into resistance, and resistance turning into support.

235
00:13:08,380 --> 00:13:11,860
First, let's look at support turning into resistance.

236
00:13:11,910 --> 00:13:15,340
Here's a strong support level.

237
00:13:15,390 --> 00:13:19,060
You can see that a major bullish move started here.

238
00:13:19,110 --> 00:13:23,800
Later, price returned to the same area, bounced again, and created

239
00:13:23,850 --> 00:13:27,840
a bullish break of structure by reaching a higher level.

240
00:13:27,890 --> 00:13:30,580
But eventually, the support broke.

241
00:13:30,630 --> 00:13:33,950
Now, what should we look for?

242
00:13:34,000 --> 00:13:37,720
Earlier, when evaluating support, we checked whether price

243
00:13:37,770 --> 00:13:40,740
had moved upward strongly from the level.

244
00:13:40,790 --> 00:13:43,460
When evaluating resistance, we checked whether a

245
00:13:43,510 --> 00:13:46,730
strong sell-side move had started there.

246
00:13:46,780 --> 00:13:50,520
When support breaks and may become resistance, we want to

247
00:13:50,570 --> 00:13:54,020
see how strong the selling was during the breakdown.

248
00:13:54,070 --> 00:13:56,550
Did sellers take control aggressively?

249
00:13:56,600 --> 00:14:00,120
Did price break an important low?

250
00:14:00,170 --> 00:14:04,300
The stronger the breakdown and the more meaningful the break of structure,

251
00:14:04,350 --> 00:14:08,750
the more interested I become in a possible retest of that level.

252
00:14:08,800 --> 00:14:10,330
Here's our example.

253
00:14:10,380 --> 00:14:12,310
Support breaks.

254
00:14:12,360 --> 00:14:16,030
Price moves sharply downward with strong selling pressure.

255
00:14:16,080 --> 00:14:18,760
Then price comes back up to test the

256
00:14:18,810 --> 00:14:21,990
old support, which may now act as resistance.

257
00:14:22,040 --> 00:14:25,690
A bearish candle appears near that level.

258
00:14:25,740 --> 00:14:28,880
I can consider a short entry with a stop loss above

259
00:14:28,930 --> 00:14:34,870
the resistance zone and a target near 4,200, a round-number level.

260
00:14:34,920 --> 00:14:37,530
Let's see how the chart develops.

261
00:14:37,580 --> 00:14:39,430
Price moves down.

262
00:14:39,480 --> 00:14:44,970
Later, it comes back up and tests that same resistance area again.

263
00:14:45,020 --> 00:14:47,730
Why am I watching this level closely?

264
00:14:47,780 --> 00:14:51,450
Because the initial breakdown had strong selling momentum,

265
00:14:51,500 --> 00:14:55,490
and price also broke structure to the downside.

266
00:14:55,540 --> 00:14:58,640
Now we wait for a bearish candle to appear.

267
00:14:58,690 --> 00:15:00,230
There it is.

268
00:15:00,280 --> 00:15:03,400
Let's say I take the short entry, place the stop

269
00:15:03,450 --> 00:15:08,930
loss above that candle, and set a target around 4,200.

270
00:15:08,980 --> 00:15:12,150
In this example, price hits our stop loss.

271
00:15:12,200 --> 00:15:14,360
We lose the trade.

272
00:15:14,410 --> 00:15:17,800
But notice that price eventually moves down afterward.

273
00:15:17,840 --> 00:15:20,370
This shows why stop-loss placement matters.

274
00:15:20,420 --> 00:15:24,260
A stop just above an individual candle may

275
00:15:24,310 --> 00:15:26,270
be too tight for the structure we're trading.

276
00:15:26,320 --> 00:15:29,330
A wider stop above the full invalidation zone would have changed the

277
00:15:29,380 --> 00:15:36,420
result, although it would also have changed the risk and position size.

278
00:15:36,470 --> 00:15:40,450
The main point here isn't the specific stop loss or target.

279
00:15:40,500 --> 00:15:44,690
It's learning how to recognize and mark the important zone.

280
00:15:44,740 --> 00:15:47,600
I hope that example makes sense.

281
00:15:47,650 --> 00:15:52,860
Now let's look at the opposite: resistance turning into support.

282
00:15:52,910 --> 00:15:56,020
Here we have another useful example.

283
00:15:56,070 --> 00:15:58,430
First, notice this support level.

284
00:15:58,480 --> 00:16:02,080
Price has found support here several times, and

285
00:16:02,130 --> 00:16:05,390
a strong move began from this area.

286
00:16:05,440 --> 00:16:08,600
But there's also a resistance level above it.

287
00:16:08,650 --> 00:16:14,390
Price has tested that resistance before, and now it finally breaks through.

288
00:16:14,440 --> 00:16:19,420
Once resistance is broken, it may start acting as support.

289
00:16:19,470 --> 00:16:21,260
What do we need to check?

290
00:16:21,310 --> 00:16:23,590
The same two questions.

291
00:16:23,640 --> 00:16:28,910
First, did price break the resistance with strong bullish momentum?

292
00:16:28,960 --> 00:16:30,670
Yes.

293
00:16:30,720 --> 00:16:33,620
Second, did the move create a bullish break of

294
00:16:33,670 --> 00:16:37,930
structure by taking out a previous significant high?

295
00:16:37,980 --> 00:16:40,350
Yes, it did.

296
00:16:40,400 --> 00:16:42,960
So we have two reasons to pay attention to this

297
00:16:43,010 --> 00:16:47,080
level when price comes back down to retest it.

298
00:16:47,130 --> 00:16:52,070
If price touches the former resistance, now potential support, and prints a

299
00:16:52,120 --> 00:16:56,860
bullish candle, that may give us an opportunity to consider a buy.

300
00:16:56,910 --> 00:16:58,640
Let's watch what happens.

301
00:16:58,680 --> 00:17:03,040
Price comes back and just touches the area.

302
00:17:03,090 --> 00:17:06,790
Suppose we wait for that bullish candle, enter a buy,

303
00:17:06,840 --> 00:17:10,230
and place the stop loss below the support zone.

304
00:17:10,280 --> 00:17:13,340
We also set our target above the entry.

305
00:17:13,390 --> 00:17:15,280
And here you can see that price

306
00:17:15,330 --> 00:17:18,140
makes a very, very strong move.

307
00:17:18,190 --> 00:17:20,880
Now you know the two key questions I

308
00:17:20,930 --> 00:17:24,290
ask when marking support and resistance zones.

309
00:17:24,340 --> 00:17:28,350
First, did price move away from that level with strength?

310
00:17:28,400 --> 00:17:32,040
Second, did that move break market structure?

311
00:17:32,090 --> 00:17:34,390
Use those two questions as part of your

312
00:17:34,440 --> 00:17:38,910
chart analysis, and always manage your risk.

313
00:17:38,960 --> 00:17:42,530
If this lesson helped you, subscribe to GoPipways,

314
00:17:42,580 --> 00:17:46,880
and start your trading journey at Gopipways.

315
00:17:46,920 --> 00:17:47,640
GoPipways.

316
00:17:47,690 --> 00:17:50,710
Trade with a plan, not a guess.
